Subject: Re: modeling log transformed values in lme (transformation bi
Message-ID: <31CFB6E0A456D511917700C04F10997C619C11@orme.nogent.cemagref.fr>

One further comment and one question to the following Bill's comment - that I find very interesting:

[WNV]  it all hinges on the error structure.  If you have a

> multiplicative error structure (in my experience by far the usual case) you

> should transform and use lme, if you have a multiplicative mean structure

> but an additive, homoscedastic error structure, you should use either nlme

> or the function glmmPQL from the MASS4 library.  I'd be surprised if you

> need the latter, but of course the former is problematical if you have some

> rogue zeros in the data.  Avoid the temptation to "add a little bit" and

> then transform and pretend nothing happened.  Please.

Comment:

Isn't there a third possibility: you have an additive mean structure that you want to keep and a multiplicative error structure? In this case, a double - on y and on x- transform strategy (cf. Carroll & Ruppert 1988)  with use of nlme might work. Isn't it?

Carroll R.J. et Ruppert D., 1988, Transformation and weighting in Regression, London, Chapman & Hall.

Question:

what would be the difference between lme and nlme in case you have a lot of zeros in a non-negative y? Don't we add more than a little bit when we tranform y in lme (e.g. log(y+1))?

Frédéric Gosselin

-----Message d'origine-----

De : David Parkhurst [mailto:parkhurs at indiana.edu]

Envoyé : jeudi 17 octobre 2002 03:47

À : wsb2 at cornell.edu; s-news at lists-new.biostat.wustl.edu

Objet : Re: [S] modeling log transformed values in lme (transformation

bias)

One other caution---if you log transform your data, be careful transforming it back to

the original scale.  That can lead to substantial downward "transformation bias,"

which seems not to be sufficiently well known.  For more on that, you might see

Parkhurst, D. F. 1998. Arithmetic versus geometric means for environmental

concentration data. Environmental Science and Technology 32: 92A-98A. and references

therein.

Dave Parkhurst

