In-Reply-To: <5.1.0.14.2.20021016150928.009fa3f0@postoffice4.mail.cornell.edu>
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20021018053253.009f8ec0@postoffice4.mail.cornell.edu>

Many thanks for the very informative answers to my question regarding
alternatives for modeling mixed random and fixed effect data which is not
normally distributed but becomes so upon log transformation. Gina
Lockwood, Bill Venables,  David Parkhurst, Gosselin Fredric, and
Joel Miller kindly responded with a very useful series of responses, each
of which seemed to elaborate  the previous response:

Gina Lockwood began by pointing out that I needed to be sure not to
confuse the log transformation with the log or logit links, and reminding
that interpretation of the coefficients of log transformed data is less
straightforward than in the original scale;

Bill Venables did a great job distinguishing multiplicative from additive
errors in the mean structure versus the errors, and went on to apply that
to choosing an appropriate modeling strategy using lme vs nlme;

David Parkhurst cautioned on a somewhat less well appreciated 
implication of using log transformation in that a downward bias is
created, and provided a helpful reference to expand that 

Gosselin Fredric elaborated on an additional possibility for the
structure/origin  of the non normality element for which an
alternative modeling strategy was suggested and provided a supporting
reference, and then raised a question  regarding zeros and non
negative y transformations

And finally, Joel Miller provided a recommendation and a rationale for
proceeding with the modeling process:

The responses are summarized in the order I received them 
below:

Gina Lockwood began by pointing out that I needed to be sure not to
confuse the log transformation with the log or logit links, and reminding
that interpretation of the coefficients of log transformed data is less
straightforward than in the original scale.

Bill Venables did a great job distinguishing multiplicative or additive
errors in the mean structure versus the errors, and went on to apply that
to choosing an appropriate modeling strategy as follows:

>         [WNV]
> I take it you mean that after log transformation of the data
>
> the residuals appear more normally distributed, after fitting
> appropriate
>
> linear models.
>
>
> >  the data is reflects both fixed and random effects, so I would
> like to
>
> > use lme. however, am I correct in that there is no capability in lme
> to
>
> > use a log-link model?
>
>         [WNV]
> No, there is not, and if your statement above is correct you
>
> don't want a log link.  With a log link you will be fitting a model
> with
>
> that has additive, homoscedastic errors but with a multiplicative
> mean
>
> structure.  It sounds like you have a multiplicative error structure
> as well
>
> which requires a log transformation of the data to turn it into an
> additive,
>
> homoscedastic one.
>
>
> > It seems my alternatives with this data are to either transform it
> to
>
> > logged values and model the logged values with lme, or to use the
> raw
>
> > values and model it with nlme?  If this is the case, which is
> the
>
> > preferable approach, using lme with log transformed values, or using
> nlme?
>
>         [WNV]
> it all hinges on the error structure.  If you have a
>
> multiplicative error structure (in my experience by far the usual case)
> you
>
> should transform and use lme, if you have a multiplicative mean
> structure
>
> but an additive, homoscedastic error structure, you should use either
> nlme
>
> or the function glmmPQL from the MASS4 library.  I'd be surprised if
> you
>
> need the latter, but of course the former is problematical if you have
> some
>
> rogue zeros in the data.  Avoid the temptation to "add a little
> bit" and
>
> then transform and pretend nothing happened.
> Please.

David Parkhurst cautioned on a somewhat less well appreciated 
implication of using log transformation in that a downward bias is
created, and provided a helpful reference to expand that 

> One other caution---if you log
> transform your data, be careful transforming it back to
>
> the original scale.  That can lead to substantial downward
> "transformation bias,"
>
> which seems not to be sufficiently well known.  For more on that,
> you might see
>
> Parkhurst, D. F. 1998. Arithmetic versus geometric means for
> environmental
>
> concentration data. Environmental Science and Technology 32: 92A-98A. and
> references
>
> therein.
>
>
> Dave Parkhurst

Gosselin Fredric elaborated on an additional possibility for the
structure/origin  of the nonnormality element, offered an
alternative modeling strategy and raised a question  regarding zeros
and non negative y transformations

> One further comment
> and one question to the following Bill's comment - that I find very
> interesting:
>
>
> [WNV]  it all hinges on the error structure.  If
