> > should transform and use lme, if you have a
> multiplicative mean structure
>
> > but an additive, homoscedastic error structure, you
> should use either nlme
>
> > or the function glmmPQL from the MASS4 library.
> I'd be surprised if you
>
> > need the latter, but of course the former is
> problematical if you have some
>
> > rogue zeros in the data.  Avoid the temptation to
> "add a little bit" and
>
> > then transform and pretend nothing happened.
> Please.
>
>
> Comment:
>
> Isn't there a third possibility: you have an additive mean
> structure that you want to keep and a multiplicative error structure? In
> this case, a double - on y and on x- transform strategy (cf. Carroll
> & Ruppert 1988)  with use of nlme might work. Isn't it?
>
>
>
> Carroll R.J. et Ruppert D., 1988, Transformation and
> weighting in Regression, London, Chapman & Hall.
>
>
> Question:
>
> what would be the difference between lme and nlme in case
> you have a lot of zeros in a non-negative y? Don't we add more than a
> little bit when we tranform y in lme (e.g. log(y+1))?
>
>
>
> Frédéric Gosselin

And finally, Joel Miller provided a recommendation and a rationale for
proceeding with the modeling process:

> If you know that a log
> transformation is appropriate, I would recommend
>
> doing the log transform first and then using lme.  The estimation
> procedure
>
> will be much simpler, and the likelihood of convergence to
> meaningful
>
> parameter estimates will be greater.
>
>
> Sincerely,
>
>
> Joel

