I've narrowed the problem down to S_alloc()s, but don't know what cause the
problem or how to get around it.  Hopefully some kind soul can help!

I've made up a dummy C function as such:

void trymem(long *n1) {
  double *x1, *x2, ... /* I declared forty such pointers, x1 to x40. */
  S_EVALUATOR
  x1 = (double *) S_alloc(*n1, sizeof(double), S_evaluator);
  ...
  x40 = (double *) S_alloc(*n1, sizeof(double), S_evaluator);
}

Then compiled it using the MinGW compiler (gcc version 2.95.3-6 (mingw
special)), and made the dll, following the instruction in the online
complement of S Programming.  When I call the C function from Splus, it
crashes after the 17th call to S_alloc().  Can anyone offer any hints as to
why?  Even better, how do I get around this?  Could it be compiler
incompatibility?

TIA,
Andy

