From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk
Date: Tue, 22 Oct 2002 10:42:13 +0100
Subject: Re: Query: output of date()
In-Reply-To: <F12D043C8E69D211B90A0010E36079C203DA21E3@mail.ilim.com>
References: <F12D043C8E69D211B90A0010E36079C203DA21E3@mail.ilim.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0210221034480.4857-100000@gannet.stats>

GDT is Windows' idea of `Greenwich Mean Time Daylight Savings Time',
which is really called `British Summer Time'.

Well, you don't expect people in Seattle to know about non-US places, do
you?

On Tue, 22 Oct 2002 Glenn.Treacy at ILIM.COM wrote:

> Hi,
>
> I am using S+6.1 on NT4. The output of date() is "Tue Oct 22 10:21:11 GDT
> 2002" but my value of options$time.zone is "GMT" and GDT does not seem to be
> a valid time zone judging from the output of timeZoneList()? As a result I
> am having difficulty converting date() to a timeDate object. Does anyone
> have an idea what is going on?

Normally the time-zone GMT indicates exactly that (no DST).  In POSIX
terms one would use GB, and in S-PLUS's sparse list, it seems to be
timeZoneC("britain").

