Thanks to Sean Keenan, Sundar Dorai-Raj, Pete Meyer, Dimitris Rizopoulos,
David Lorenz, and Samuel Buttrey, I, now, have a better idea of solving the
problem. Given below is my original posting and their responses.

Calvin

My Query:
A simple parabolic fit to a dataset containing 5 values using 'nls' results
in an error. Could someone please explain what I am doing wrong?
  > d <- data.frame(x = c(11, 12, 13, 14, 15), y = c(68, 64, 63, 65, 70))
  > fit1 <- nls(d$y ~ a * d$x^2 + b * d$x + c, data = d, start = list(a = 1,
b = -30, c = 300))
  Problem in nls(d$y ~ a * d$x^2 + b * d$x + c, data = d, start = list(a..:
step factor reduced below minimum 
  Use traceback() to see the call stack
When I try this in Excel, I get y = 1.5x^2 -38.5x + 310 with an R^2 value of
1.
I am running S-PLUS 6.1 in Windows 2000 Professional.

The Responses:
Hi Calvin;
If you get an r-squared of 1 in excel, then you must have a deterministic
relationship.  Try adding a little noise to your dependent variable and see
if nls() runs.  Also, your equation is only non-linear in one dimmension and
that variable is only squared.  Why not create d$x2<-d$x^2 and use lm()?
i.e. fit1 <- lm(d$y ~ a * d$x2 + b * d$x + c, data = d).
Cheers,
Sean Keenan        

Calvin,
  nls() is for nonlinear fits in the parameters not in the regressors as
defined in your problem. Try lm() instead:
fit1 <- lm(y ~ poly(x, 2), data = d)
which fits b0 + b1 * x + b2 * x^2, so note that the parameters (b0, b1,
b2) are still linear. An example where nls would be better suited would
be in fits such as
y ~ b0 + b1 * exp(- b2 * x)
where (b1, b2) are now nonlinear with respect to y.
Regards,
Sundar

Calvin,
  Ignore the code from before. Don't know what I was thinking. Try:
lm(y ~ x + x^2, data = d)
instead.
Regards,
Sundar

Hi Calvin!
I don't know the whole answer, but I can at least tell you how to
see what is happening.  First, I would put in at least one decimal
point in each sequence so that Splus sees things as numeric rather
than integers, then issue the following commands:
d2 <- data.frame(x = c(11., 12, 13, 14, 15), y = c(68., 64, 63, 65, 70))
fit2 <- nls(y ~ a * x^2 + b * x + c, data = d2, start = list(a = 1.,
    b = -30., c =
300.),control=nls.control(minscale=.00001,tol=.00001,trace=T))
The control argument tells Splus to give you the trace of its solution
(which still fails).  But what you will see is:
> fit2 <- nls(y ~ a * x^2 + b * x + c, data = d2, start = list(a = 1., b
= -30., c = 300.),
    control = nls.control(minscale = 1e-005, tol = 1e-005,trace = T))
1331 : 1 -30 300
2.98884e-026 : 1.5 -38.5 310
1.13091e-026 : 1.5 -38.5 310
4.03897e-027 : 1.5 -38.5 310
Problem in nls(y ~ a * x^2 + b * x + c, data = d2, start = list(a = 1., b
= -30., c = 3..: step factor reduced below minimum
Use traceback() to see the call stack
Of course, you've seen the last of the message before.  If you look at the
documentation for nls.control, you'll see the following:
OPTIONAL ARGUMENTS:
  maxiter
  maximum number of iterations during fitting. Default 50.
  tolerance
  tolerance for the relative offset convergence criterion in the algorithm.
Default 0.001. Note that the convergence test used in nls() is strictly
relative. Therefore if the solution to a problem turned out to be a perfect
fit (unlikely except in artificial examples), convergence is not guaranteed
to be recognized by the algorithm.
  minscale
  minimum factor by which to shrink the default step size in an attempt to
decrease the sum of squares. Default 0.001.
Under tolerance the situation you are facing is described.  I'm not sure why
it fails to recognize that it has found the answer.  I played with tolerance
and
minscale a little, but couldn't get it to recognize convergence, although
the
trace indicates that it found the solution.
Hope that helps.
Pete Meyer

Dear Calvin,
The model you are describing is a simple linear model:
d <- data.frame(x = c(11, 12, 13, 14, 15), y = c(68, 64, 63, 65,
70))
fit <- lm(y~x+I(x^2), data=d)
coef(fit)
I hope this helps.
Best,
Dimitris

Calvin,
  The problem is probably related to the fact that you actually have a
linear equation.  Try this:
fit2 <- lm(y ~ x^2 + x, data = d)
Dave

Hi. nls() is not very good about curves that fit exactly. If you run it with
trace=T you'll see it find the correct solution before giving up. The reason
it gives up is that the objective function is so small. Note this line in
the help for nls.control: "Therefore if the solution to a problem turned out
to be a perfect fit (unlikely except in artificial examples), convergence is
not guaranteed to be recognized by the algorithm."
Does this help?
Sam Buttrey

